            "y": 84
          },
          "id": 33,
          "options": {
            "content": "| Metric family | Where it lives | What it answers | Bad sign |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `telegram.file.queue.assets` | Current State, Backlog And Bytes | How many durable file assets are known, ready, or failed. | `failed > 0` or `known` grows while jobs do not move. |\n| `telegram.file.queue.jobs` | Current State, Backlog And Bytes | How many download jobs are queued or already handed to TDLib. | `queued` grows or `downloading` stays high without ready/failed outcomes. |\n| `telegram.file.queue.bytes` | Backlog And Bytes | Known downloaded, remaining, and total bytes for active jobs. | Remaining bytes grows while worker job rate is flat. |\n| `telegram.file.queue.unknown_remaining` | Backlog And Bytes | Active jobs whose size is unknown, so byte panels undercount backlog. | High value means the byte x-ray is incomplete. |\n| `telegram.file.worker.wake` | Worker Flow | Why the worker was scheduled. | `file_download_defer` or `failure_backoff` dominates. |\n| `telegram.file.worker.jobs` | Current State, Worker Flow | What happened to jobs after the worker touched them. | `failed` or `stale` dominates `ready`. |\n| `telegram.file.worker.stage.duration` | Current State, Worker Flow | How often each worker stage runs and where worker time is spent. | High `dispatch_tdlib`, `canonicalize_completed`, or `publish_changes`. |\n| `telegram.file.record.stage.duration` | File Discovery And Record Path | Time spent discovering, materializing, pruning, and publishing file slots before downloads exist. | High `replace_slots` or `publish_changes`. |\n| `rpc.server.call.duration` filtered to file procedures | Boundaries And Storage | File-facing Dashboard request rate and latency. | High `requestFile` points to enqueue/policy/DB; high `file` points to serving ready files. |\n| `db.client.operation.duration` filtered to file tables | Boundaries And Storage | DB load and latency from file queue, asset, slot, and TDLib file tables. | High rate with high p95 means file storage is the DB pressure source. |\n| `messaging.client.operation.duration` filtered to `telegram.files.*` | Boundaries And Storage | Rate and latency of publishing file queue/owner state changes. | High p95 means state changes are slow before subscribers see them. |\n| `messaging.process.duration` filtered to `telegram.files.*` | Boundaries And Storage | Rate and latency of subscribers applying file queue/owner events. | Send rate without process rate, or high process p95. |",
            "mode": "markdown"
          },
